==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|malison}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|maledictiō}}, from the past participle stem of {{m|la|maledīcō||I speak ill of}}, from {{m|la|male||wickedly, badly}} + {{m|la|dīcō||say, speak}}. Compare {{m|en|malediction}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmælɪzən/|/ˈmælɪsən/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[curse]], a [[malediction]].
#* {{quote-book|year=1819|passage=A hide of land I give to thee in my steads of Walbrugham, from me and mine to thee and thine aye and for ever; and God’s '''malison''' on his head who this gainsays!|author=Walter Scott|title=Ivanhoe}}
#* '''1837''' {{w|Thomas Carlyle}}, ''{{w|The French Revolution: A History}}''
#*: Rascality male and female is prowling in view of him. His fasting stomach is, with good cause, sour; he perhaps cannot forbear a passing '''malison''' on them; least of all can he forbear answering such.

